A race condition is when the outcome depends on the unpredictable timing of two or more processes touching the same resource, so the result is correct most of the time and wrong occasionally. With a shared log, the symptom is interleaved or garbled lines when two scripts write at once.

To avoid it, take an exclusive lock around each append with `flock`:

```sh
log() {
    {
        flock -x 9
        printf '%s %s\n' "$(date -Is)" "$*" >&9
    } 9>>/var/log/shared.log
}

log "backup started"
```

Every writer must use the same lock (here, the log file itself), or the lock protects nothing. Always open the log with `>>`, never `>`. Append mode makes the kernel seek to the end on every write, whereas `>` truncates the file and lets writers overwrite each other.

A single short line written with `>>` is already atomic on a local filesystem, so the lock matters mainly for multi-line or large entries. On NFS neither append nor `flock` is reliable, so there have one process own the file, for example by sending messages through `logger` to syslog.
